.TH tuxlsx n 0.1 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tuxlsx
.PP
Minimal Excel (\fB.xlsx\fR, Office Open XML) helpers in pure Tcl.
.PP
\fBtuxlsx\fR writes one sheet of rows and reads the cell values of any workbook back. It is not a spreadsheet toolkit: no formats, formulas, column widths or several sheets when writing; no formatting when reading.
.SS Package
.CS
package require tclutils::tuxlsx 0.1
.CE
.SS API
.CS
::tclutils::tuxlsx::writeRows xlsxFile rows ?-sheet NAME? ?-numbers auto|0? ?-title T? ?-creator C?
::tclutils::tuxlsx::sheets xlsxFile
::tclutils::tuxlsx::rows xlsxFile ?-sheet NAME|INDEX?
::tclutils::tuxlsx::text xlsxFile
::tclutils::tuxlsx::part xlsxFile name
::tclutils::tuxlsx::parts xlsxFile
.CE
.IP \(bu
\fBwriteRows\fR writes ROWS (a list of rows, each a list of cell values) into one sheet (\fB\-sheet\fR, default \fBSheet1\fR). With \fB\-numbers auto\fR (default) a value that looks like a decimal number (\fB\-12\fR, \fB3.5\fR, \fB1e3\fR) is stored as a number, everything else as text; \fB\-numbers 0\fR stores every cell as text. \fB007\fR and \fB1,5\fR stay text. Text is written inline, not as shared strings. A minimal \fBstyles.xml\fR is included, so Excel opens the file without a repair message. Returns the file name.
.IP \(bu
\fBsheets\fR returns the sheet names in workbook order.
.IP \(bu
\fBrows\fR returns the cells of one sheet (\fB\-sheet\fR by name or 1\-based number, default the first) as a list of rows. Values as Excel stores them: shared and inline strings as text (rich text joined, phonetic runs left out), numbers as written in the file (a date is its serial number, a percentage its fraction), booleans \fB1\fR/\fB0\fR, formulas their last computed value, errors as text (\fB#DIV/0!\fR). Gaps are filled with \fB""\fR; a row is as long as its last cell, a missing row is an empty list.
.IP \(bu
\fBtext\fR returns all sheets: cells joined by \fB\et\fR, rows by \fB\en\fR, sheets separated by an empty line.
.IP \(bu
\fBpart\fR returns one container part as text, \fBparts\fR the member names.
.PP
Errors: \fB{TCLUTILS TUXLSX SHEET name}\fR unknown sheet; \fB{TCLUTILS TUXLSX VALUE}\fR a sheet name Excel does not accept (empty, more than 31 characters, one of \fB[ ] : * ? / \e\fR, starting or ending with \fB'\fR); \fB{TCLUTILS TUXLSX PART name}\fR a missing part; \fB{TCLUTILS COMMON OPTION ...}\fR bad options. A missing or damaged ZIP is reported by \fBtuzip\fR.
.SS Example
.CS
package require tclutils::tuxlsx

::tclutils::tuxlsx::writeRows devices.xlsx {{Item Count} {Printer 3} {Router 1}} -sheet Devices
::tclutils::tuxlsx::sheets devices.xlsx                 ;# -> Devices
::tclutils::tuxlsx::rows devices.xlsx                   ;# -> {Item Count} {Printer 3} {Router 1}
.CE
.SS Notes
.PP
Output is deterministic (fixed timestamp). Excel, LibreOffice and openpyxl open the files. The reader is a small scanner, not an XML parser; it reads what Excel, LibreOffice and openpyxl write. Command line: \fBbin/tuxlsx.tcl sheets|rows|text|create\fR.
